 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Establishes a program to provide education and training to individuals fifty years of age and older regarding the transition to an entrepreneur and small business developer.",
 "title": "Establishes a program to provide education and training to individuals fifty years of age and older regarding the transition to an entrepreneur and small business developer",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A7206",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A7206-2019",
     "printNo": "A7206",
     "session": 2019,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 153\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bills:\n \nSenate Bill Number 232, entitled:\n \n    \"AN ACT to amend the economic development law and the public service\n      law,  in relation to small business energy assistance and advocacy\n      services\"\n \nSenate Bill Number 1087, entitled:\n \n    \"AN ACT to amend the economic development law, the  New  York  state\n      urban  development  corporation  act  and  the  executive  law, in\n      relation to small business crime prevention services\"\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 7206, entitled:\n \n    \"AN ACT to amend the economic development law, in relation to estab-\n      lishing a program to provide education and training to individuals\n      fifty years of age and older regarding the transition to an entre-\n      preneur and small business developer\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  Two of these bills would establish programs within  the  Division  for\nSmall  Business of the Department of Economic Development (DED). In both\ncases, the Department lacks the staff, expertise and funding to  support\nsuch programming.\n \n  The first bill would seek to create a small business energy assistance\nand  advocacy services program within the Division for Small Business of\nthe DED with an intent to create a \"one stop\" energy assistance  program\nat  the  Department for entrepreneurs to learn about, compare and select\nenergy alternatives that will  best  fit  their  operations  and  enable\ncontinued growth. Rather than DED, other state entities, such as the New\nYork  State  Energy  Research and Development Authority (NYSERDA), would\nhave greater expertise and the capacity to provide the required services\nnecessary to support such a program.\n \n  The second bill would create a new  small  business  crime  prevention\nservices program, with the advice and assistance of New York State Divi-\nsion  of  Criminal  Justice  Services (NYSDCJS), to provide, among other\nthings, information on strategies, best practices and programs  offering\ntraining  and assistance in prevention of crimes in and around the prem-\nises of small businesses or otherwise affecting small businesses. Again,\nDED lacks the requisite expertise and would be required to obtain  addi-\ntional staff to administer the program.\n \n  In  addition,  the  bill  intends  to  create  a  small business crime\nprevention assistance program providing loans and grants to small  busi-\nnesses,  municipalities,  not-for-profit corporations or other organiza-\n \ntions for the purpose of preventing crimes against small  businesses  or\non  the premises or in the vicinity of small businesses.  The program is\nproposed to be funded through the Economic Development Fund, a fund that\nis  generally agreed to be reserved by both the Executive and the Legis-\nlature  for  flexible,  responsive,  experimental  economic  development\ngrants  rather  than specific programmatic goals, and is fully committed\nfor other significant ESD projects.\n \n  The third bill would require the DED to develop education and training\nprograms and services in each economic development region of  the  state\nto  support  individuals  50  years  of age and older who are seeking to\nbecome entrepreneurs and/or small business  developers  and  to  provide\nsuch information on DED's website.\n \n  The  requirements  of  this  bill  would  be  duplicative  of existing\nprograms.   For  example,  the  Urban  Development  Corporation  already\nprovides these services and meets these needs through its Entrepreneuri-\nal  Assistance  Centers,  which provide instruction, training, technical\nassistance and support services to new and aspiring entrepreneurs of all\nages and in local communities statewide. This bill,  nonetheless,  would\ncreate  a  separate  and  duplicative  economic  development  assistance\nprogram, creating significant new  and  unfunded  costs  to  the  State,\noutside the budget process.\n \n  While  all  three  bills  have  advanced worthy purposes, because they\nwould impose programmatic constraints for funding  by  creating  signif-\nicant  new  and unfunded costs and, as such, should be considered in the\ncontext of the budget. I am, therefore, constrained to veto these bills.\n \n  These bills are disapproved.                (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
